    {
      "id": "scene_1NVHlpVlkm",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 2,
      "title": "Basic HTML Document Structure",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_xtn9g2uP",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_eOV-ENBD"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_DfPRxnHT",
          "text": "Alright, everyone, let’s dive into the basic structure of an HTML document. This is the foundation for every web page you’ll create.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_52Jwyqhl",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_bq-H33nB"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_rf1mHkU_",
          "text": "First, we start with the doctype declaration <!DOCTYPE html> — it tells the browser that this is an HTML5 document. Next comes the root <html> element, which wraps everything else. Inside <html> we have two main sections: the <head> and the <body>.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_q2aF0vZe",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_bq-H33nB"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_6oTCcn8N",
          "text": "Within the <head>, you typically place metadata like the <title> tag and <meta> tags for character encoding or viewport settings. The <body> is where all the visible content—text, images, links—goes.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_87dMdQ_C",
          "text": "So, to recap: doctype, root <html>, then <head> with title and meta, and finally <body> with the page’s actual content. Any questions before we move on?",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "qsZLfEiY3ybk42_qO6BO_",
          "theme": {
            "shadow": {
              "h": 0,
              "v": 0,
              "blur": 10,
              "color": "#000000"
            },
            "outline": {
              "color": "#d14424",
