    {
      "id": "scene_5msMm7WXOJ",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 4,
      "title": "Links and Images",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_-ISRLLX3",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Sp8P-PaX"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_dhjZQQ1L",
          "text": "Alright, everyone, let’s dive into today’s topic: Links and Images.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_zIItN5zj",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_99g_EVBG"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_-oYsw7ti",
          "text": "First, creating a hyperlink is as simple as using the <a href=\"…\"> tag. You place the URL inside the href attribute, and the linked text goes between the opening and closing tags.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_9SkdErK-",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_99g_EVBG"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_jl2EoaMd",
          "text": "Next, to embed an image, we use the <img src=\"…\" alt=\"…\"> tag. The src points to the image file, and the alt attribute provides a description for screen readers.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_78VTpl2q",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_99g_EVBG"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_CWx1EDWU",
          "text": "Remember, writing clear, descriptive alt text is crucial for accessibility—it helps users who rely on assistive technology understand what the image shows.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_efEsRlfb",
          "text": "That covers the basics of links and images. Try adding a hyperlink and an image to your own page, and we’ll review your code together.",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "vooqUnpvcv8_PgJXCKocD",
