    {
      "id": "scene_4XnHldsuIC",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 6,
      "title": "Lists and Tables",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_LAgLrIso",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_Q5uizPPN"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_KcNdf4YZ",
          "text": "Alright everyone, let's dive into Lists and Tables. We'll see how to create ordered and unordered lists, and then build a simple table.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_A1oU9DVT",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_eqYgJb7Y"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_vbBangHB",
          "text": "First, an ordered list uses the <ol> tag together with <li> for each item. This automatically numbers the items for you.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_4CA9wqvT",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_eqYgJb7Y"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_tU0zj5y9",
          "text": "Next, an unordered list uses the <ul> tag with <li> items, giving you bullet points instead of numbers.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_A7Kr5LZf",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_eqYgJb7Y"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_tBxcI_VE",
          "text": "Now, let's look at building a simple table. We start with the <table> element, then add rows with <tr>, header cells with <th>, and data cells with <td>.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_MHvUG7Oe",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_eqYgJb7Y"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_RyJ5EIDP",
          "text": "Great job! Remember, lists help organize information, and tables let you compare data neatly. Any questions before we move on?",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
        "canvas": {
          "id": "x4XrQJLgQuDYxMHubQTST",
