    {
      "id": "scene_1OSd1SEAo9",
      "type": "slide",
      "order": 7,
      "title": "Forms Basics",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "action_swNG6rsu",
          "type": "spotlight",
          "elementId": "text_yNuGPmvX"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_NlDrOV0u",
          "text": "Alright everyone, let’s dive into the basics of HTML forms. We'll see how to gather user input and make our pages interactive.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_yBi6Xh7X",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_Um1Nf2m3"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_IW5L7iXe",
          "text": "First, every form starts with a <form> container. The action attribute tells the browser where to send the data when the user submits the form.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_rgJ3tCC4",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_Um1Nf2m3"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_dsTFe8bW",
          "text": "Next, inside the form we use common input elements like <input> for single‑line data, <textarea> for longer text, and <button> to trigger submission.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_CiBr_pBA",
          "type": "laser",
          "elementId": "text_Um1Nf2m3"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_WdW0kF5-",
          "text": "Finally, pairing each input with a <label> improves accessibility, letting screen readers and users know exactly what information is required.",
          "type": "speech"
        },
        {
          "id": "action_D2BxYx1X",
          "text": "That covers the three core pieces: the form container with its action, the basic input elements, and proper labeling for accessibility. Any questions before we move on?",
          "type": "speech"
        }
      ],
      "content": {
        "type": "slide",
